ion of their struggle. But the person with severe coprolalia? Their videos are shared precisely *because* the symptom is so shocking, unexpected, and clickable. The algorithm feeds you more of what grabs attention, creating a feedback loop where your entire digital exposure is to the most extreme and least common manifestation. It would be like judging all weather by watching nothing but tornado footage and concluding it's always catastrophic outside. **3. Confirmation bias kicks in.** Once the "swearing disease" stereotype is set in your mind, every time you see someone cussing and then find out they have Tourette's, it reinforces the rule. But you never have the opposite experience—no one walks up to you and says, "Just so you know, I have Tourette syndrome, and all I do is clear my throat." The silent cases never add their data point to your collection. So your personal "dataset" becomes completely lopsided. To use a simple analogy: You might say, "Every single person I've ever seen having an epileptic seizure has been on the floor convulsing, so that must be what epilepsy always looks like." And you'd be right about what you witnessed, but you'd be missing the millions of people with absence seizures who just stare blankly for ten seconds—because you'd never know that's what you were seeing. So, your experience is absolutely real and valid—you really *have* seen those people, and they really do have Tourette's. I'm not trying to erase them. But the interpretation that this is "everyone" with the condition is a perfect storm of attention bias, selective exposure, and the dramatic nature of the symptom itself. I hope that makes the 10-15% figure feel less like a contrarian lie and more like a truth hiding in plain sight.
